[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Variables and Constants in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, variables and constants are fundamental building blocks for storing and manipulating data. Understanding their differences and proper usage is crucial for writing effective and maintainable code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Variables"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A variable is a named storage location in memory that can hold a value. The value stored in a variable can be changed during the execution of a program. In C#, you must declare a variable before you can use it. Declaration involves specifying the variable's data type and name."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Variable declaration
int age; // Declares an integer variable named 'age'
string name; // Declares a string variable named 'name'
bool isEmployed; // Declares a boolean variable named 'isEmployed'
double salary; // Declares a double variable named 'salary'"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"After declaring a variable, you can assign a value to it using the assignment operator (=)."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Variable assignment
age = 30; // Assigns the value 30 to the 'age' variable
name = "John Doe"; // Assigns the string "John Doe" to the 'name' variable
isEmployed = true; // Assigns the boolean value 'true' to the 'isEmployed' variable
salary = 50000.00; // Assigns the double value 50000.00 to the 'salary' variable"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also declare and initialize a variable in a single statement."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Declaration and initialization
int age = 30;
string name = "John Doe";
bool isEmployed = true;
double salary = 50000.00;"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Types"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# is a strongly-typed language, meaning that every variable must have a specific data type. Some common data types include:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"int: Represents integers (whole numbers) e.g., -1, 0, 100."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"double: Represents floating-point numbers (numbers with decimal points) e.g., 3.14, -2.5."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"string: Represents a sequence of characters (text) e.g., "Hello", "C#"."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"bool: Represents a boolean value (true or false)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"char: Represents a single character e.g., 'A', '5'."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Constants"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A constant is a named storage location in memory that holds a value that cannot be changed after it is initialized. Constants are useful for representing values that are known at compile time and should not be modified during program execution. To declare a constant, use the 'const' keyword."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"// Constant declaration and initialization
const double PI = 3.14159; // Declares a constant named 'PI' with the value 3.14159
const int MAX_SIZE = 100; // Declares a constant named 'MAX_SIZE' with the value 100
const string GREETING = "Hello, World!"; // Declares a constant string"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attempting to modify a constant after it has been initialized will result in a compile-time error."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Invalid operation (will cause a compile-time error)
// PI = 3.14; // Cannot assign to 'PI' because it is a 'const' variable"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Readonly Fields"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'readonly' keyword is used to define a field that can only be assigned a value during declaration or within the constructor of the class. Unlike constants, 'readonly' fields can have their values determined at runtime."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"public class MyClass
{
    public readonly int MyReadOnlyField;

    public MyClass(int value)
    {
        MyReadOnlyField = value; // Initialized in the constructor
    }
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key differences between 'const' and 'readonly': 'const' values must be known at compile time, while 'readonly' values can be determined at runtime. 'readonly' fields can be initialized in the constructor, allowing for different values based on constructor parameters."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Naming Conventions"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# has certain naming conventions that are recommended for variables and constants to improve code readability:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Variable names should be descriptive and start with a lowercase letter (camelCase). For example: 'firstName', 'age', 'isEmployed'."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Constant names should be in uppercase with words separated by underscores (PascalCase). For example: 'MAX_SIZE', 'PI', 'DEFAULT_NAME'."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Scope of Variables"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"The scope of a variable determines where in the code it can be accessed. Variables declared within a block of code (e.g., inside a method or loop) are only accessible within that block. Variables declared outside of any block (e.g., as class members) have a wider scope."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"public class Example
{
    int classVariable = 10; // Class-level variable

    public void MyMethod()
    {
        int methodVariable = 20; // Method-level variable

        if (true)
        {
            int blockVariable = 30; // Block-level variable
            Console.WriteLine(classVariable); // Accessible
            Console.WriteLine(methodVariable); // Accessible
            Console.WriteLine(blockVariable); // Accessible
        }

        Console.WriteLine(classVariable); // Accessible
        Console.WriteLine(methodVariable); // Accessible
        // Console.WriteLine(blockVariable); // Error: Not accessible outside the 'if' block
    }
}"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding variable scope is essential to avoid naming conflicts and ensure that variables are used correctly within your program."}]
